Convolution / SAC
-----------------
The SAC convolve_ command is effectively the same as the command
correlate_ excpet for a sign change.  As with correlate_, the
calculation is done in the frequency domain.  An alternative
convolution routine, which operates in the time domain, is introduced
in the following section.

.. code-block:: c

    void convolve(float *a, int na, float *b, int nb, float *c, int nc)

Convolve two time series together

**Arguments**

- `a` - First time series
- `na` - Length of a
- `b` - Second time series
- `nb` - Length of b
- `c` - Output convolution of a and b, must be at least na+nb-a length
- `nc` - Length of c, must be at least na+nb-1

**Examples**

.. code-block:: c

    #define NMAX 2020
    int na, nb, nerr, max, n;
    float beg, delta, ya[NMAX], yb[NMAX], yc[2*NMAX];

    max = NMAX;
    // Read in the first file
    rsac1("data.sac", ya, &na, &beg, &delta, &max, &nerr, SAC_STRING_LENGTH);

    // Read in the second file
    rsac1("triangle.sac", yb, &nb, &beg, &delta, &max, &nerr, SAC_STRING_LENGTH);

    // Convolve the two time series
    convolve(ya, na, yb, nb, yc, na+nb-1);



**Effective SAC Commands**

.. code-block:: shell

    SAC> fg triangle delta 1e-2 npts 100
    SAC> write triangle.sac
    SAC> fg seismo
    SAC> write data.sac
    SAC> read data.sac triangle.sac
    SAC> convolve


Convolution / Time Domain
-------------------------

The SAC convolve_ command is a "discrete" convolution.  It uses two
digital-convolution features that may be undesirable when working with
time series:

  - no scaling of the output by the digitizing interval, and
  - no check on the start time for the pulse.

This independent program allows for both discrete convolution (as
is done by the SAC convolve) and a time-series convolution.

The programs with examples can be found in $SACHOME/doc/examples/convolve

**Sample Runs**

Input for the convolution can be generated as::

    SAC> fg triangle npts 8 delta 0.02 begin -0.08
    SAC> write triangle_n8_d0.02.sac
    SAC> fg impulse npts 12 delta 0.02 begin 0
    SAC> write impulse_n12_d0.02.sac

Then run the convolvef program::

    % ./convolvef
    Usage convolvef p_name wf_name c_name disc_conv
    % ./convolvef triangle_n8_d0.02.sac impulse_n12_d0.02.sac conv_f_n.sac n

The above gives a time-series convolution.  The pulse is symmetric
around zero time, so comparing the last argument "y" and "n"
for disc_conv shows an
important difference between discrete and time-series convolutions.

Time-Series Convolution Primer
------------------------------

In these application, :math:`f`, is a waveform time series that is
convolved with a pulse :math:`g`.  The equation for their convolution
is

.. math::

   y(t) = f(t) \star g(t) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dt' f(t') g(t-t')

Both :math:`f` and :math:`g` are functions of time, and their zero times are coupled
through the term :math:`g(t−t′)`. From the above equation, it is easy to show that
one can choose the time zero for :math:`y` and :math:`f` to be the same. In the
applications discussed below, the zero time for :math:`g(t)` can be the same
as or less than the zero time for :math:`f(t)`.

To calculate the convolution, one discretizes both :math:`f` and
:math:`g` and replaces the integral with a sum. In this discussion
:math:`\delta t= 0.02s` is the digitizing interval for :math:`y`,
:math:`f`, and :math:`g`. One multiplies the sum by :math:`\delta t`,
which is not what is done in a “discrete convolution”, which also does
not take into account any difference in zero time between :math:`f(t)`
and :math:`g(t)`.

Here, two applications of convolution are discussed. Both have the
same :math:`f(t)` but different :math:`g(t)`.

:math:`f(t)` is a synthetic waveform (produced using Haskell matrices
or WKBJ) where vertical lines of calculated polarities and amplitudes
are drawn at phase-arrival times. For these examples, the synthetic
waveform is a vertical-component time series for an incident P-wave at
an angle of :math:`20^\circ` with the vertical through a 2-layer
crust. There are :math:`n_w = 2048` points in the discretized
:math:`f`. (The large number is chosen to minimize wraparound.) The
duration is :math:`t_w = \delta t [n_w-1]`. The first point in
:math:`f` is chosen to be at :math:`t=0`, so :math:`f(t)
=f(t)H(t)H(t_w−t)`,where

.. math::

   H(t) = \begin{cases}
              1 & \text{if } t \ge 0 \\
              0 & \text{if } t < 0
          \end{cases}

:math:`g(t)` is a pulse waveform with a far smaller duration than
:math:`f(t)`. Here, the pulse is either (1) an approximation of a P
arrival so that the output of the convolution potentially models the
data, or (2) a time-symmetric shape with a maximum at :math:`t=0` to
smooth out the Gibbs phenomenon that often accompanies arrivals in
synthetics. Option (2) is often used for synthetics for receiver
functions.

A “Brune” pulse for (1): :math:`g(t) =U_0 H(t) t e^{−t/\kappa}
H(t_p−t)`, where :math:`\kappa=0.1s`, :math:`t_p=1.26s`, and
:math:`U_0` is a constant that only affects the amplitude, so is of no
interest here.

For (2), the source pulse is a triangle function produced by fg in SAC::

   fg triangle npts 8 delta 0.02 begin -0.08.

Note that the Brune pulse starts at the same time as :math:`f`, while the triangle pulse starts at
a negative time with a maximum at the start time for :math:`f`. If the total time for the pulse
is :math:`t_p` , the general form for the pulse is :math:`g(t) =g(t)H(t−t_1)H(t_2−t)`, where for the Brune pulse :math:`t_1=0`, :math:`t_2=t_p`
and for the triangle pulse :math:`t_1=−0.08s`,  :math:`t_2 = 0.06s = t_p−t_1`. For either pulse, :math:`t_p=\delta t[n_p−1]`.

Given the above forms for :math`f` and :math:`g` , the original convolution equation can be written

.. math::

   y(t) = \int_0^{t_w} dt' f(t') g(t-t') H(t-t'-t_1) H(t_2 - t + t')

For the Brune pulse, :math:`t_1=0`, so :math:`t` cannot be negative, but for the triangle pulse the lower bound for
:math:`t` is :math:`-0.06 s`. In Fortran, arrays are stored in the computer using positive integers, so to simplify
the bookkeeping it is best to avoid negative times when we discretize the above equation. One can
avoid negative times if one chooses :math:`y(\tau) =y(t−t_1)`. With this choice the modified equation then reads

.. math::

   y(\tau) = y(t-t_1) = \int_0^{t_w} dt' f(t') g(\tau + t_1 -t') H(\tau - t') H(t_2 - \tau + t_1 + t')

The discretized equation for the above is then

.. math::

   y_i = \delta t \sum _{j=1}^{n_w} f_j g_{i-j-j_1}

where :math:`j_1 = -(t_1/\delta t) + 1` and :math:`i` runs from :math:`1` to :math:`n_w + n_p - 1`

The following Fortran code produces the correct result for the convolution for either pulse

.. code-block:: fortran

        j_1 = -nint(b_p/delta)+1
        do i=1,n_w+n_p-1
          temp = 0.0
          do j=1,n_w
            if (i.ge.(j-j_1) .and. n_p.ge.(i-j+j_1)) then
                temp = temp + waveform(j)*pulse(i-j+j_1)
            endif
          end do
          conv(i) = delta*temp
        end do

where b_p = :math:`t_1`, delta = :math:`\delta t`, and conv(i) = :math:`y_i`.

Plots for the two pulse waveforms are shown in Figure 1, and the results of the convolution near the first synthetic arrival are shown in Figure 2 both for the Brune pulse and for the triangle pulse. The Gibbs phenomenon is quite pronounced at the arrival in the raw synthetic.  Note that the peaks for the arrival in Figure 2 are at the same time for the triangle-pulse convolution and the synthetic, and the Brune-pulse convolution starts at the peak of the raw-synthetic arrival time. For display purposes, the waveforms are  time-shifted so that the first arrival are at five seconds into the record.  Hence the zero time for the display and the convolution calculation are not the same.

If one uses the SAC convolution for the two runs, one gets the "correct" result for the Brune pulse but a time shift of 0.08 seconds for the triangle pulse (which starts at -0.08 s).  The convolution of the triangle pulse with itself is also time-shifted 0.08\,s.  If one uses the SAC CONVOLVE option "amplitude on" in program `convolvef` or `convovlec`, one gets the same amplitudes and times as for SAC convolution output.


